module Darklang.Cli.Docs.TerminalUI

let content () : String =
  """# Terminal UI

Every full-screen thing in the CLI - the workbench, nav, the editor, the outliner - and the inline
prompt and completion picker all render through one layer, `Darklang.Cli.Tui`. This is how it works
and, more usefully, why it doesn't flicker.

## The model

Three layers, and the separation is the whole point:

1. A view is a value.
   `Tui.View = { rows: List<String>; cursor: Cursor; mode: ViewMode }`. Rows are plain text plus SGR
   styling only: no cursor movement, no clearing, no mode changes. Zero-based coordinates throughout.

2. A frame is a diff.
   `Frame` / `InlineFrame` compare the previous view to the next one and produce one string of
   terminal updates.

3. A session owns the effects.
   `TerminalSession` holds `{ status; previousView; terminalSize }`, decides full-repaint vs diff,
   writes exactly once, and knows how to restore the terminal.

  packages/darklang/cli/tui/
    core.dark             View, Size, Cursor, ViewMode  (types only)
    text.dark             widths, clipping, wrapping, SGR sanitizing
    layout.dark           blankRows / fitRows / viewport
    frame.dark            fullscreen diffing, absolute addressing
    inlineFrame.dark      inline diffing, cursor-relative addressing
    terminalSession.dark  lifecycle, alt-screen, restore guards
    terminalSupport.dark  "can we even run a TUI here"

`ViewMode` is the fork that matters:

  Fullscreen  takes the alternate screen, addresses rows absolutely (ESC [ row;1 H).
              The workbench, nav, the editor, caps, outliner, review.
  Inline      occupies N rows where the cursor already is, keeps normal scrollback, and addresses
              rows relative to where the previous frame left the cursor (ESC [ n A / ESC [ n B).
              The main prompt and the completion picker.

## Why it doesn't flicker

Five independent mechanisms. If you are writing a view, you get all of them for free; if you are
changing the renderer, these are the things not to break.

1. Nothing is cleared wholesale.
   Clearing with `ESC [ 2J` and repainting is the flash: the terminal genuinely blanks first. The only
   `2J` in the CLI is the explicit `clear` command; no render path clears the screen.

2. Only changed rows are emitted.
   `Frame.changedRows` walks the previous and next row lists together and emits a change only where
   they differ. Rows that vanished are emitted as empty so they get erased. If nothing changed and
   the cursor didn't move, the render returns "" and nothing is written at all. Arrowing down a list
   rewrites two rows.

3. One buffered write per frame.
   Every render path builds a single string and hands it to one `Stdlib.print`. No
   `printLine`-per-row, so there is no window where a half-drawn frame is visible.

4. Synchronized output markers.
   Each non-empty update is wrapped in `ESC [ ?2026h` ... `ESC [ ?2026l`. Terminals that support DEC
   mode 2026 present the update atomically; the rest ignore the two sequences.

5. The cursor is hidden across the update.
   Updates begin with `ESC [ ?25l` and end by either re-showing at the target position or staying
   hidden, so you never watch the cursor skate around mid-repaint.

The per-row sequence is worth reading carefully, because the ordering is load-bearing:

  fullscreen:  ESC[{row};1H  ESC[0m  ESC[K  {content}  ESC[0m  \r
  inline:      \r            ESC[0m  ESC[K  {content}  ESC[0m

- Erase BEFORE writing, never after. Erasing after wipes the final cell of a full-width row, because
  the terminal is parked in deferred-wrap state on that cell.
- The reset precedes the erase, so blank cells don't inherit a background colour.
- The trailing reset closes any style the row left open.
- The trailing \r cancels deferred wrap before the next update.

## Measuring text

`Tui.Text.displayWidth` counts terminal columns; `styledWidth` does the same while ignoring SGR
sequences. Use them for anything positional.

`Stdlib.String.length` is not the tool for column math. It counts extended grapheme clusters, which
is the right answer for "how many characters is this" and the wrong one for "how far does this reach
across the screen": `界` is one grapheme occupying two columns, and so is a ZWJ emoji. Because a Dark
`Char` is a grapheme cluster, clipping can't split a flag or a family emoji in half.

## Rules for a view

1. Rows contain text and SGR only. Everything else belongs to the renderer.
2. View functions are pure. If you need the world, read it during the state transition and stash the
   result on the state. The renderer may call your view function and diff it.
3. One write per frame. Don't interleave `printLine` with a live session.
4. Measure with `Tui.Text.displayWidth` for plain text, `styledWidth` once SGR is in the row - never
   `Stdlib.String.length`. Passing a row containing control sequences to `displayWidth` is not valid.
5. Release the inline region before printing to scrollback. `releasePromptRegion` /
   `stopInlineWithView` shrink the region to what should persist and hand the terminal back;
   printing over a live inline region corrupts it.
6. Present on transitions, not on every loop pass.

## Safety and degradation

- Dynamic content is sanitized on the way out: clipping a row (`Text.normalizeToWidth` ->
  `clipToWidth`, native) keeps only numeric SGR and discards every other CSI, and anything else that
  looks like an escape loses its ESC byte and renders as ordinary text. So a package name or
  description can't move your cursor or reset your terminal. The renderer is the single boundary.
- `TerminalSession.armRestoreGuard` registers a restore sequence with the host before entering a
  surface, so a hard kill still un-wrecks the terminal.
- `TerminalSupport.current ()` refuses to emit control sequences when stdin or stdout isn't a tty, or
  `TERM` is unset / dumb / unknown. Piping the CLI prints a clean sentence rather than escape soup.
- Resize is handled: SIGWINCH sets a flag, the input read polls for it, and a resize reports a key no
  view handles, which sends the loop round to re-sample the size and repaint.
"""
